  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nThe lake effect rain showers are slowly coming to an end this\nafternoon. The latest radar trends have indicated a decrease in\nthe coverage and intensity of the lake effect showers over the\npast few hours. A large and high pressure system currently over\nthe Cornbelt of the Midwest will slowly build over the Ohio\nValley and Great Lakes region tonight through much of this\nweek. Unfortunately the clouds will take some more time break \nup and scattered out. Some partial clearing of the clouds will \nstart late tonight or by sunrise Monday morning over northwest \nOhio. The clearing line should progress eastward across NEOH and\nNWPA during the day Monday with most of the area seeing some \nlate day sunshine during the afternoon. Tuesday looks to be a\nvery sunny day with clear skies and seasonable temperatures.\nHigh temperatures Monday and Tuesday will generally be in the\nlow to mid 50s and overnight low temps in the 30s. \n\nA large upper level ridge will develop over the central U.S. \nand Midwest early this week and slowly move eastward into the \nOhio Valley and Great Lakes region through mid-week. The upper\nlevel ridge aloft and the large high pressure system at the\nsurface together will keep our weather pattern very quiet and\nrainfree for much of this week. Temperatures will gradually\nclimb and be above average by 10 to 15 degrees towards the\nlater half of this week into the weekend. High temperatures\nThursday through Saturday will be in the upper 50s to middle 60s\nacross the area. Overnight temps will be in the 40s. The next\nchance of possible rainfall looks to be late next weekend.",
